{% if include_ai and ai_backend != "memory" %}"""The finance analyst agent: a local model that narrates, never decides.

Anomaly detection is deterministic and lives in ``categorize/insights.py``.
This module supplies the other half: a factual snapshot of an owner's finances,
and the agent definition that turns that snapshot into a short daily note.

The split is the whole design. Rules decide what is unusual; the model only
puts it into words. Every figure the agent can write is one this module already
computed, so a wrong answer is a badly worded answer rather than a fabricated
alert. That is what makes a small local model safe to point at real money.

Two pieces ship here:

- ``finance_snapshot`` - a memory-module fetcher, registered at import time.
  A ``memory_module`` row names it, the ``finance-analyst`` agent opts into
  that module, and the context renderer runs it per turn.
- The fixtures that create both rows.

``aegis init`` seeds these through ``load_all_ai_fixtures``. A project that
added the AI service later has empty registry tables and can seed them in
place with the same call the generator makes::

    uv run python -c "from app.core.db import SessionLocal; \
from app.services.ai.fixtures import load_all_ai_fixtures; \
load_all_ai_fixtures(SessionLocal())"
"""

